WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.394 --> 00:00:03.746
[MUSIC]

00:00:07.709 --> 00:00:12.580
What are some of the most common myths
about the Stanford application process?

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:15.296
&gt;&gt; I think the myth that I
hear most frequently is that

00:00:15.296 --> 00:00:19.630
members of the Admissions Committee at
Stanford have some magic checklist that

00:00:19.630 --> 00:00:23.329
each applicant must fulfill in order
to be admitted to our program.

00:00:23.329 --> 00:00:26.821
I've heard things like,
I have to have a GMAT score over 700, or

00:00:26.821 --> 00:00:29.761
I need to work in a particular
industry to be admitted, or

00:00:29.761 --> 00:00:33.041
even come from a particular type
of educational institution.

00:00:33.041 --> 00:00:35.648
And really,
nothing could be farther from the truth.

00:00:35.648 --> 00:00:39.791
If you think about the last few classes
that we've admitted, we have students from

00:00:39.791 --> 00:00:44.114
over 60 different countries, we have
students from a 160 different educational

00:00:44.114 --> 00:00:48.257
institutions, and we have students coming
from, I think, on average over 300

00:00:48.257 --> 00:00:51.897
different work environments before
they come, different companies.

00:00:51.897 --> 00:00:54.343
And there's only 400
students in [LAUGH] a class.

00:00:54.343 --> 00:00:58.930
So you can see, in reality, we're
admitting humans for what they've done and

00:00:58.930 --> 00:00:59.760
who they are.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.602
We're not admitting people just because
they went to a certain institution or

00:01:03.602 --> 00:01:05.123
worked in a certain industry.

00:01:05.123 --> 00:01:07.791
In fact, I was just,
I think it was a few weekends ago,

00:01:07.791 --> 00:01:11.155
we were at Admitted Students Weekend,
which I know you've been to,

00:01:11.155 --> 00:01:14.125
Admit Weekend, and
there was two students I was talking to.

00:01:14.125 --> 00:01:18.406
And both of them said,
they almost didn't apply to Stanford.

00:01:18.406 --> 00:01:20.060
Like, I came this close to not applying.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:21.070
I'm like, why?

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:25.245
One said he was in chatroom and somebody
told him that Stanford will never admit

00:01:25.245 --> 00:01:29.260
you because the institution, [COUGH]
because the university that you went to.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:32.937
And he went to a state
school in the Midwest.

00:01:32.937 --> 00:01:36.180
And then another student said
the same thing had happened to her.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:38.284
And one of her peers
from work had told her,

00:01:38.284 --> 00:01:41.853
they were talking about where they
wanted to apply, that with her GPA and

00:01:41.853 --> 00:01:44.213
test scores,
she'd never get into Stanford.

00:01:44.213 --> 00:01:48.534
So luckily for us, both of these students
decided that, for themselves, whether or

00:01:48.534 --> 00:01:51.787
not they should apply, and
are now going to be entering this fall.

00:01:51.787 --> 00:01:54.079
So we're really excited about that.

00:01:54.079 --> 00:01:57.400
I think what's most important
to me is to tell you,

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:00.194
do not underestimate your own potential.

00:02:00.194 --> 00:02:02.119
There's no perfect applicant.

00:02:02.119 --> 00:02:05.886
There's no checklist that we're looking
at of attributes that we require.

00:02:05.886 --> 00:02:09.419
Decide for yourself whether
you're worth the investment to

00:02:09.419 --> 00:02:11.641
go through the application process.

00:02:11.641 --> 00:02:15.669
And what I know from talking to many, many
applicants in the past, that you spending

00:02:15.669 --> 00:02:19.642
the time to know whats important to you,
what you value, what you're aspirations

00:02:19.642 --> 00:02:23.389
are, and then what you've really done and
the accomplishments you've had,

00:02:23.389 --> 00:02:27.770
those are going to be critical to you at
this point in time no matter what you do.

00:02:27.770 --> 00:02:30.610
So we're genuinely excited
to learn about you.

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:34.410
To learn about how you've actually
had impact on your teams and

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:37.490
your organizations and
your communities and what you care about.

00:02:37.490 --> 00:02:40.330
So if you have a story to tell us,
you know that we're here and

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:42.330
genuinely ready and excited to read it.

